###
post: Direct J and K, recomputing the integrals instead of storing them
agent: claude

The Fock matrix needs the Coulomb and exchange matrices of a density `D`:

    J[i, j] = sum_kl (ij|kl) D[k, l]
    K[i, j] = sum_kl (ik|jl) D[k, l]

With the integrals stored in packed-8 form this is a loop over the vector.
The vector grows as `n^4 / 8`: 18.4 MB at 65 AOs and 172 MB at 114. Direct
J/K stores nothing. Every SCF iteration recomputes each shell quartet with
`mderi_block` and adds it into J and K straight away, then throws it away.

**One unique integral, eight contributions.** `mderi_block` gives each
integral once, for `i >= j`, `k >= l`, `ij >= kl`. Its 8 index orders all
carry the same value, and each order adds to J and K in its own place:

    order (p q | r s)   adds  g D[r, s] to J[p, q]   and  g D[q, s] to K[p, r]

When indices coincide (`i = j`, `k = l`, or `ij = kl`), some of the 8 orders
are the same order counted twice, so `g` is halved once for each
coincidence before the 8 contributions are added. The result is exact for any
`D`, symmetric or not.

**Parallel, with the same answer on any thread count.** The quartets come from
the same flat list as `mderi_build`, and they are dealt round-robin into
`MDJK_NCHUNK` = 32 chunks. A `parallel for` runs over the chunks. Each chunk
adds into its own copy of J and K, and after the loop the copies are summed
in chunk order. A chunk handles the same quartets in the same order whichever
thread runs it, so the default build, the OpenMP build and any
`OMP_NUM_THREADS` give the same bits.

The signature is the frozen J/K one, `(X1, X2, X3, D, opts) -> [2 ndens, n, n]`
with `atoms`, `shells` and `prims` in `X1 .. X3`, so this member can sit in
the same registry as the in-core ones. `opts[OPT_SCREEN_TOL]` is the Schwarz
threshold. Everything is over unit-normalized Cartesian AOs.

**Density screening** (`mdjk_direct_screened`, the production member). A
quartet adds `g D` terms to J and K, with `|g| <= Q_AB Q_CD`, so a quartet
whose bound times the largest density element it meets is small adds
nothing that matters. With `Dm[A, B]` the largest `|D|` over the functions
of shells A and B (and every density), the quartet `(AB|CD)` is skipped when

    Q_AB Q_CD max(4 Dm[A,B], 4 Dm[C,D], Dm[A,C], Dm[A,D], Dm[B,C], Dm[B,D])

is at or below `opts[OPT_DIRECT_TOL]`, 1e-13: the rule and the threshold of
PySCF's direct SCF (`CVHFnrs8_prescreen` with `direct_scf_tol = 1e-13`; the
4 is J's weight). On a full density this drops few quartets. On the change
of density between two SCF iterations, which the incremental Fock build of
lib/hf/scf_loop hands it, it drops most of them once the SCF is close to
converged. The Schwarz factors `Q` are a property of the basis: a caller
that builds J and K many times computes them once (`mderi_schwarz`) and
passes them (`mdjk_screened_q`). The quartets are dealt into the chunks by
bra pair, so no quartet list is built per call; `mdjk_direct`, which deals
the Schwarz list round-robin and screens by nothing else, checks it.

Used with `import hf.md_direct`.
###

from hf.opts import OPT_DIRECT_TOL, OPT_SCREEN_TOL
from hf.md_eri import (
    mderi_block, MDERI_D_GA, MDERI_D_GB, MDERI_D_NA, MDERI_D_NB, MDERI_D_SA, MDERI_D_SB,
    MDERI_DIR_LEN, mderi_grid, mderi_groups, mderi_qlist, mderi_schwarz, mderi_store,
    mderi_store_seg, MDERI_TOP,
)
from hf.basis import bas_nao_cart, SH_CART0, SH_NCART
from hf.eri_store import es_get, es_rng

MDJK_NCHUNK = 32

#| Add block `B` of store pairs `(k1, k2)` into the J and K copies of one
#| chunk, `W[w0 + 2d]` (J) and `W[w0 + 2d + 1]` (K) for each density `d`.
#| Rows and columns of `B` are numbered as lib/hf/md_eri's packs number
#| them, a shell pair of the two groups at a time.
#@ 69e0ba72
def mdjk_put(W: Arr, w0, B: Arr, D: Arr, nd, ST: Arr, shells: Arr, k1, k2) {
    d1 = MDERI_TOP + MDERI_DIR_LEN * k1
    d2 = MDERI_TOP + MDERI_DIR_LEN * k2
    sa = ST[d1 + MDERI_D_SA]
    sb = ST[d1 + MDERI_D_SB]
    sc = ST[d2 + MDERI_D_SA]
    sd = ST[d2 + MDERI_D_SB]
    gna = ST[d1 + MDERI_D_NA]
    gnb = ST[d1 + MDERI_D_NB]
    gnc = ST[d2 + MDERI_D_NA]
    gnd = ST[d2 + MDERI_D_NB]
    na = shells[sa, SH_NCART]
    nb = shells[sb, SH_NCART]
    nc = shells[sc, SH_NCART]
    ndd = shells[sd, SH_NCART]
    for ga to gna {
        for ia to na {
            i = shells[sa + ga, SH_CART0] + ia
            for gb to gnb {
                for ib to nb {
                    j = shells[sb + gb, SH_CART0] + ib
                    if sa == sb and j > i {
                        continue
                    }
                    ij = i * (i + 1) / 2 + j
                    row = ((ga * gnb + gb) * na + ia) * nb + ib
                    for gc to gnc {
                        for ic to nc {
                            k = shells[sc + gc, SH_CART0] + ic
                            for gd to gnd {
                                for id to ndd {
                                    l = shells[sd + gd, SH_CART0] + id
                                    if sc == sd and l > k {
                                        continue
                                    }
                                    kl = k * (k + 1) / 2 + l
                                    if k1 == k2 and kl > ij {
                                        continue
                                    }
                                    #: halve once per equal index pair, so the eight orders count each integral once
                                    f = B[row, ((gc * gnd + gd) * nc + ic) * ndd + id]
                                    if i == j {
                                        f = 0.5 * f
                                    }
                                    if k == l {
                                        f = 0.5 * f
                                    }
                                    if ij == kl {
                                        f = 0.5 * f
                                    }
                                    for d to nd {
                                        wj = w0 + 2 * d
                                        wk = wj + 1
                                        dkl = f * (D[d, k, l] + D[d, l, k])
                                        dij = f * (D[d, i, j] + D[d, j, i])
                                        W[wj, i, j] = W[wj, i, j] + dkl
                                        W[wj, j, i] = W[wj, j, i] + dkl
                                        W[wj, k, l] = W[wj, k, l] + dij
                                        W[wj, l, k] = W[wj, l, k] + dij
                                        W[wk, i, k] = W[wk, i, k] + f * D[d, j, l]
                                        W[wk, j, k] = W[wk, j, k] + f * D[d, i, l]
                                        W[wk, i, l] = W[wk, i, l] + f * D[d, j, k]
                                        W[wk, j, l] = W[wk, j, l] + f * D[d, i, k]
                                        W[wk, k, i] = W[wk, k, i] + f * D[d, l, j]
                                        W[wk, l, i] = W[wk, l, i] + f * D[d, k, j]
                                        W[wk, k, j] = W[wk, k, j] + f * D[d, l, i]
                                        W[wk, l, j] = W[wk, l, j] + f * D[d, k, i]
                                    }
                                }
                            }
                        }
                    }
                }
            }
        }
    }
    return 0
}

#| One quartet of the list into chunk `c`'s copies; a def of its own so the
#| block is freed as soon as it has been added.
#@ 005d2ee4
def mdjk_one(W: Arr, D: Arr, nd, ST: Arr, G: Arr, shells: Arr, QL: Arr, q, c) {
    k1 = QL[q, 0]
    k2 = QL[q, 1]
    mdjk_put(W, 2 * nd * c, mderi_block(ST, k1, k2, G), D, nd, ST, shells, k1, k2)
    return 0
}

#| Chunk `c` of `nch`: quartets `c, c + nch, c + 2 nch, ...` of the list,
#| added into `W`, the chunk's own copies of J and K.
#@ 39acc072
def mdjk_chunk(W: Arr, D: Arr, nd, ST: Arr, G: Arr, shells: Arr, QL: Arr, nq, c, nch) {
    q = c
    while q < nq {
        mdjk_one(W, D, nd, ST, G, shells, QL, q, 0)
        q = q + nch
    }
    return 0
}

#| J and K with the quartets dealt into `nch` chunks. The result does not
#| depend on the thread count; it depends on `nch` only through the order of
#| the final sums.
#@ 53fe5f5d
def mdjk_direct_nch(atoms: Arr, shells: Arr, prims: Arr, D: Arr, opts: Arr, nch) {
    n = bas_nao_cart(shells)
    nd = len(D)
    ST = mderi_store_seg(atoms, shells, prims)
    G = mderi_grid(ST)
    npl = ST[0]
    tol = opts[OPT_SCREEN_TOL]
    Q = mderi_schwarz(ST, G)
    QL = mderi_qlist(Q, npl, tol)
    nq = len(QL)
    # pass c fills a copy of its own and files it as slab c of W, so the
    # passes write disjoint slabs
    W = zeros(nch, 2 * nd, n, n)
    parallel for c to nch {
        Wc = zeros(2 * nd, n, n)
        mdjk_chunk(Wc, D, nd, ST, G, shells, QL, nq, c, nch)
        setslab(W, c, Wc)
    }
    #: summed in chunk order, so the result does not depend on threads
    out = zeros(2 * nd, n, n)
    for c to nch {
        Wc = slab(W, c)
        for r to 2 * nd {
            setslab(out, r, slab(out, r) + slab(Wc, r))
        }
    }
    return out
}

#| The J/K registry member: `X1 .. X3` = atoms, shells, prims; `D` is
#| `[ndens, n, n]`; the result is `[2 ndens, n, n]`, J then K per density.
#@ d52d5288
def mdjk_direct(X1: Arr, X2: Arr, X3: Arr, D: Arr, opts: Arr) -> Arr {
    return mdjk_direct_nch(X1, X2, X3, D, opts, MDJK_NCHUNK)
}

#| The largest `|D|` over the functions of each pair of shell groups
#| (`GT`, lib/hf/md_eri's `mderi_groups`) and every density, `[ng, ng]`,
#| symmetric. A group's functions are consecutive.
#@ 6ff6be83
def mdjk_dmax(D: Arr, shells: Arr, GT: Arr) {
    ng = len(GT)
    A = abs(slab(D, 0))
    for d from 1 to len(D) {
        A = max(A, abs(slab(D, d)))
    }
    lo = zeros(ng)
    hi = zeros(ng)
    for g to ng {
        lo[g] = shells[GT[g, 0], SH_CART0]
        hi[g] = lo[g] + GT[g, 1] * shells[GT[g, 0], SH_NCART]
    }
    Dm = zeros(ng, ng)
    for a to ng {
        for b to a + 1 {
            v = max(max(A[lo[a]:hi[a], lo[b]:hi[b]]), max(A[lo[b]:hi[b], lo[a]:hi[a]]))
            Dm[a, b] = v
            Dm[b, a] = v
        }
    }
    return Dm
}

#| One quartet computed and added into `W`; a def of its own so the block is
#| freed as soon as it has been added.
#@ 27d38d0b
def mdjk_pair(W: Arr, D: Arr, nd, ST: Arr, G: Arr, shells: Arr, k1, k2) {
    mdjk_put(W, 0, mderi_block(ST, k1, k2, G), D, nd, ST, shells, k1, k2)
    return 0
}

#| Chunk `c` of `nch` of the screened build: bra pairs `c, c + nch, ...`,
#| each against every ket pair up to it, a quartet kept when it passes the
#| Schwarz test (`Q_AB Q_CD >= tol_s`) and the density test (header), with
#| `Dm` over the store's shell groups.
#@ e3033706
def mdjk_chunk_screened(W: Arr, D: Arr, nd, ST: Arr, G: Arr, shells: Arr, Q: Arr, Dm: Arr, tol_s, tol_d, c, nch) {
    #: bounds for the early bra test
    npl = ST[0]
    qmax = max(Q)
    dmax = max(Dm)
    #: skip a bra pair no ket can lift over the density test
    k1 = c
    while k1 < npl {
        ga = ST[MDERI_TOP + MDERI_DIR_LEN * k1 + MDERI_D_GA]
        gb = ST[MDERI_TOP + MDERI_DIR_LEN * k1 + MDERI_D_GB]
        q1 = Q[k1]
        # no ket pair can lift this bra pair over the density test
        if q1 * qmax * 4.0 * dmax > tol_d {
            dab = 4.0 * Dm[ga, gb]
            for k2 to k1 + 1 {
                q = q1 * Q[k2]
                if q >= tol_s {
                    gc = ST[MDERI_TOP + MDERI_DIR_LEN * k2 + MDERI_D_GA]
                    gd = ST[MDERI_TOP + MDERI_DIR_LEN * k2 + MDERI_D_GB]
                    dm = max(max(dab, 4.0 * Dm[gc, gd]), max(max(Dm[ga, gc], Dm[ga, gd]), max(Dm[gb, gc], Dm[gb, gd])))
                    if q * dm > tol_d {
                        mdjk_pair(W, D, nd, ST, G, shells, k1, k2)
                    }
                }
            }
        }
        k1 = k1 + nch
    }
    return 0
}

#| J and K of `D` with Schwarz and density screening, from the Schwarz
#| factors `Q` of the store's pairs (`mderi_schwarz`); `[2 ndens, n, n]`.
#@ 58c7efa4
def mdjk_screened_q(atoms: Arr, shells: Arr, prims: Arr, D: Arr, opts: Arr, Q: Arr) -> Arr {
    n = bas_nao_cart(shells)
    nd = len(D)
    nch = MDJK_NCHUNK
    ST = mderi_store(atoms, shells, prims)
    G = mderi_grid(ST)
    Dm = mdjk_dmax(D, shells, mderi_groups(shells, prims))
    tol_s = opts[OPT_SCREEN_TOL]
    tol_d = opts[OPT_DIRECT_TOL]
    #: one J/K copy per chunk: parallel without shared writes
    W = zeros(nch, 2 * nd, n, n)
    parallel for c to nch {
        Wc = zeros(2 * nd, n, n)
        mdjk_chunk_screened(Wc, D, nd, ST, G, shells, Q, Dm, tol_s, tol_d, c, nch)
        setslab(W, c, Wc)
    }
    #: summed in chunk order, so the result does not depend on threads
    out = zeros(2 * nd, n, n)
    for c to nch {
        out = out + slab(W, c)
    }
    return out
}

#| The density-screened J/K registry member: `X1 .. X3` = atoms, shells,
#| prims. It computes the Schwarz factors itself; the SCF's direct member
#| (lib/hf/jk_direct) takes them from the basis blob instead.
#@ 50faef85
def mdjk_direct_screened(X1: Arr, X2: Arr, X3: Arr, D: Arr, opts: Arr) -> Arr {
    ST = mderi_store(X1, X2, X3)
    return mdjk_screened_q(X1, X2, X3, D, opts, mderi_schwarz(ST, mderi_grid(ST)))
}

#| Test helper: `nd` seeded symmetric densities `[nd, n, n]` with entries in
#| (-1, 1), from the Park-Miller generator of `lib/hf/eri_store`.
def mdjk_rand_dens(nd, n, seed) {
    st = zeros(1)
    st[0] = seed
    D = zeros(nd, n, n)
    for d to nd {
        for i to n {
            for j to i + 1 {
                v = 2.0 * es_rng(st) - 1.0
                D[d, i, j] = v
                D[d, j, i] = v
            }
        }
    }
    return D
}

#| Test helper: J and K of `D` by dense contraction of the packed vector,
#| as two `[n^2, n^2]` matrix-vector products: `J = Gj @ vec(D)` with
#| `Gj[(i,j),(k,l)] = (ij|kl)`, and `K = Gk @ vec(D)` with
#| `Gk[(i,j),(k,l)] = (ik|jl)`. The largest gap to `JK` over every entry is
#| returned.
def mdjk_dense_gap(Gp: Arr, JK: Arr, D: Arr, n) {
    #: (ij|kl) for J, (ik|jl) for K, as dense matrices
    Gj = zeros(n * n, n * n)
    Gk = zeros(n * n, n * n)
    for i to n {
        for j to n {
            for k to n {
                for l to n {
                    Gj[i * n + j, k * n + l] = es_get(Gp, i, j, k, l)
                    Gk[i * n + j, k * n + l] = es_get(Gp, i, k, j, l)
                }
            }
        }
    }
    w = 0.0
    for d to len(D) {
        v = zeros(n * n)
        for k to n {
            for l to n {
                v[k * n + l] = D[d, k, l]
            }
        }
        J = Gj @ v
        K = Gk @ v
        for i to n {
            for j to n {
                w = max(w, abs(JK[2 * d, i, j] - J[i * n + j]))
                w = max(w, abs(JK[2 * d + 1, i, j] - K[i * n + j]))
            }
        }
    }
    return w
}

